0025-gpui-webview-devtools.patch (2026-11-16, built and verified).

Applies with `patch -p1` at the root of a copy of nsh-host-allspeech-src after patches 0010-0024, in order.
Verified: applies cleanly on top of 0024 (checked against a reverted copy of the three touched files, byte-for-byte
identical to the applied result), and `cargo check --release --offline -p nsh-platform-gpui` (from
`third_party/nsh-host-combined-src/crates/gpui`) succeeds with no warnings and no new Cargo.lock entries.

gpui: webview DevTools (Safari Web Inspector) for native preview tabs

Why: DESKTOP_PARITY_AUDIT.md's "DevTools" item said this was NOT POSSIBLE because neither `WebViewHostProps` nor
`WebViewRef` exposed an inspector and nsh-host-src had no `isInspectable`/`openDevTools` binding to gate a row on.
This patch adds that binding: `webview.devtools.setInspectable { enabled }` and `webview.devtools.open {}`, so the
reference's "Open DevTools" row in the preview "more" menu can be wired up instead of staying permanently absent.

Two calls, two different API tiers, documented rather than blurred together (`native/devtools.rs`'s module doc has
the full reasoning):

- `webview.devtools.setInspectable { enabled }` sets the PUBLIC `WKWebView.inspectable` property (macOS 13.3+).
  This is Apple's documented, supported way to let Safari's Develop menu (or any `WKWebView`-hosting app's own
  inspector) attach to a web view, and it is also what makes AppKit add "Inspect Element" to the view's native
  context menu. Guarded with `-respondsToSelector:` so pre-13.3 hosts resolve the call's rejection instead of
  crashing on an unknown selector.
- `webview.devtools.open {}` calls `-[WKWebView _showInspector]`, an UNDOCUMENTED, PRIVATE WebKit selector (not in
  any public header, recovered from WebKit's own source, not from Apple documentation). It is not covered by any
  API-stability promise and can be renamed or removed by any WebKit update without notice. Shipped because it
  works today and is the only way to open the inspector window programmatically (there is no public
  `WKWebView.showInspector()`); guarded the same way with `-respondsToSelector:` so a WebKit that drops it fails
  soft (a rejection), not a crash. This is the "ship it if feasible, document it as private" half of the task; the
  TS side treats it as best-effort, not a guaranteed capability.

Scope, decided the same way 0022 (cookies) and 0024 (appearance) decide theirs: this host's web views have no
stable per-instance id a `host.call` could address (`webview.capture`, patch 0015, addresses one by on-screen
point, not a stable id). So both calls act on every `WKWebView` `native/web.rs`'s `shown()` reports *right now*,
not one tab; a tab opened afterwards starts uninspectable again until the next `setInspectable` call. Documented,
not silently narrowed away, in `native/devtools.rs`'s module doc.

Built/verified: `cargo check --release --offline -p nsh-platform-gpui` from `crates/gpui`, clean, no warnings.
NOT verified: whether `_showInspector` actually opens a visible inspector window on a real host (no WKWebView was
mounted in this check — same limitation 0022's and 0024's "Built/verified" sections note for their own calls), and
whether `isInspectable` truly adds "Inspect Element" to the context menu on this host's AppKit build. If
`_showInspector` ever stops responding (a WebKit update renames or removes it), `webview.devtools.open` degrades to
a clean rejection per view, never a crash; `setInspectable` is unaffected since it is public API.

